  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a fully ionized benzamidine-carboxylate zwitterion (logD -7.48, neutral fraction 0); direct human L1 analogs with the same amidine-carboxylate motif have F 2-3% (DX-9065a) or below 3% (peramivir), and a benzamidine dicarboxylate was not orally viable in humans.",
      "card_ids": ["C05","C10","C23","C04","C07","C26","C39"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 Caco-2 records for benzamidine compounds are uniformly low: pentamidine prodrug 2 AP-BL 1.86 x 10^-8 cm/s, pentamidine 9.3 x 10^-7, DB75 3.8 x 10^-7 with BL-AP 8.5 x 10^-7, FX-93 0.45 x 10^-6, and CRC 220 3.1 x 10^-7 without bile-salt enhancer; FX-93 solubility is favorable (0.3-18.3 mg/mL) but permeability remains the limiting factor.",
      "card_ids": ["C163","C202","C187","C166","C189","C194","C164","C188","C160","C183"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 records for carboxylate zwitterions are also low: enalaprilat PAMPA below 0.01 x 10^-6 with no detectable flux, rat jejunum absorption much lower and not concentration dependent, dog lower intestine P_eff 0.018 x 10^-4; lisinopril Caco-2 0.22-1.72 x 10^-6 with only 25% human absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C172","C179","C171","C154","C193","C169"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The query combines both permeability liabilities (benzamidine plus free carboxylate plus secondary amine), and the guanidine-carboxylate inogatran record (absorptive Papp 0.34 x 10^-6) is consistent, so the L4 measured permeability evidence affirmatively supports F below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C180"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The SQ-29852 record is the main caveat: low Caco-2 permeability (0.2 nm/sec) yet 60% human absorption, showing Caco-2 can underestimate some zwitterionic ACE inhibitors; however it lacks the benzamidine and does not overturn the consistent low-permeability pattern.",
      "card_ids": ["C178"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "high",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C163","C202","C187","C166","C189","C194","C164","C172","C179","C171","C154","C169","C180","C188","C178"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No fed-state-specific permeability or F data for the query; L4 assays used standard buffers and the only FeSSIF record (C155) concerns desmopressin, not the query class.",
    "Transporter-mediated absorption, gut-wall efflux, and intestinal metabolism are not yet characterized for the query (L5 not seen).",
    "The SQ-29852 case (C178) shows Caco-2 can underestimate some zwitterions, so human F remains uncertain without clinical data."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low: the query is a fully ionized benzamidine-carboxylate zwitterion (logD -7.48, neutral fraction 0). L1/L2 direct human analogs with the same amidine-carboxylate motif have F 2-3% (DX-9065a) or below 3% (peramivir), and a benzamidine dicarboxylate was not orally viable in humans. New L4 permeability records show uniformly low Caco-2 permeability for benzamidine compounds (pentamidine prodrug 1.86 x 10^-8, DB75 3.8 x 10^-7, FX-93 0.45 x 10^-6 cm/s) and for carboxylate zwitterions (enalaprilat PAMPA below 0.01 x 10^-6, lisinopril 0.22-1.72 x 10^-6 with 25% human absorption). The query combines both liabilities, so F below 20% is affirmatively supported. The SQ-29852 low-permeability high-absorption caveat does not overturn the consistent pattern."
}

